WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.317
Java gives you two ways to run a task over and over: scheduleAtFixedRate and scheduleWithFixedDelay.

2
00:00:09.317 --> 00:00:13.486
Give both 100 milliseconds, and they sound like the same thing.

3
00:00:13.486 --> 00:00:15.669
So what's the difference, really?

4
00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:21.572
Picture a restaurant that hires a cleaner to wipe the tables during dinner.

5
00:00:21.572 --> 00:00:24.576
The manager can give one of two instructions.

6
00:00:24.576 --> 00:00:27.780
By the clock: start a round at 6:00, 6:10, 6:20.

7
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.785
Or rest after each round: when you finish, rest ten minutes, then go again.

8
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.612
On a quiet evening, a round takes about a minute, and you can't tell the two apart.

9
00:00:38.612 --> 00:00:42.061
One second round starts at 6:10, the other at 6:11.

10
00:00:42.061 --> 00:00:42.940
Close enough.

11
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:44.042
Let's map it.

12
00:00:44.042 --> 00:00:48.014
The cleaner is a repeating task, and each round is one run.

13
00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:50.437
By the clock is scheduleAtFixedRate.

14
00:00:50.437 --> 00:00:53.668
Rest after each round is scheduleWithFixedDelay.

15
00:00:53.668 --> 00:00:55.957
And the ten minutes is the period.

16
00:00:56.667 --> 00:01:02.185
Then someone knocks over a pot of soup, and the second round takes 25 minutes.

17
00:01:02.185 --> 00:01:04.307
By the clock, it ends at 6:35.

18
00:01:04.307 --> 00:01:06.571
The third round was due at 6:20.

19
00:01:06.571 --> 00:01:07.915
When does it start?

20
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.786
At 6:35, the moment the soup round ends.

21
00:01:13.786 --> 00:01:18.569
The 6:30 round is late too, so it starts right after, at 6:36.

22
00:01:18.569 --> 00:01:22.581
Then comes 6:40, and the timetable is back on track.

23
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.931
Two rounds back to back, like a cleaner jogging from table to table to make up for lost time.

24
00:01:28.931 --> 00:01:30.579
So fixed rate catches up.

25
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.677
The resting cleaner has no timetable to catch up with.

26
00:01:34.677 --> 00:01:38.052
Its soup round starts at 6:11 and ends at 6:36.

27
00:01:38.052 --> 00:01:42.002
Ten minutes of rest, and the next round starts at 6:46.

28
00:01:42.002 --> 00:01:44.299
Every later round moves with it.

29
00:01:44.299 --> 00:01:46.382
Fixed delay never catches up.

30
00:01:46.382 --> 00:01:47.459
It just shifts.

31
00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:50.642
Now swap ten minutes for 100 milliseconds.

32
00:01:50.642 --> 00:01:54.680
One slow run of 250 milliseconds, and every other run 10.

33
00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.356
That's the lesson's model, and it gives exactly these start times.

34
00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:01.008
And on a real clock?

35
00:02:01.008 --> 00:02:06.343
The lesson's RateVsDelayDemo ran the fixed rate version on a busy machine.

36
00:02:06.343 --> 00:02:09.443
Runs 3 and 4 started 18 milliseconds apart.

37
00:02:09.443 --> 00:02:16.724
From run 5, the starts are back on the 100 millisecond grid, give or take the machine's own lateness.

38
00:02:17.533 --> 00:02:18.912
Why does this happen?

39
00:02:18.912 --> 00:02:21.143
It's one rule inside the executor.

40
00:02:21.143 --> 00:02:26.722
A repeating task goes back in its queue only when a run returns, with a new due time.

41
00:02:26.722 --> 00:02:29.806
Fixed rate adds the period to the old due time.

42
00:02:29.806 --> 00:02:32.038
Fixed delay adds the delay to now.

43
00:02:32.038 --> 00:02:36.304
And a due time in the past means run as soon as a thread is free.

44
00:02:36.533 --> 00:02:38.616
Here the cleaner picture breaks.

45
00:02:38.616 --> 00:02:41.934
A real manager might send a second cleaner to help.

46
00:02:41.934 --> 00:02:43.431
A scheduler never will.

47
00:02:43.431 --> 00:02:49.743
The task isn't back in the queue until its run ends, so it never runs at the same time as itself.

48
00:02:49.743 --> 00:02:51.500
A slow task just runs late.

49
00:02:52.333 --> 00:02:53.142
So which one?

50
00:02:53.142 --> 00:03:01.473
Fixed rate when the timing is the point, like a heartbeat, a short "still here" message that another program expects every 30 seconds.

51
00:03:01.473 --> 00:03:05.265
Fixed delay when the task calls something that can slow down.

52
00:03:05.265 --> 00:03:10.301
A service that just got over a slow spell doesn't need a burst of catch-up calls.

53
00:03:11.033 --> 00:03:12.171
Back to the soup.

54
00:03:12.171 --> 00:03:16.857
One evening, the cleaner slips on it, goes home, and never comes back.

55
00:03:16.857 --> 00:03:18.530
Nobody tells the manager.

56
00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:20.404
The tables just get dirtier.

57
00:03:21.133 --> 00:03:23.403
Java's scheduler can do the same.

58
00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:32.481
The lesson's SilentDeathDemo runs a health check every 100 milliseconds, and its third run throws an exception that nothing catches.

59
00:03:32.481 --> 00:03:33.719
What happens next?

60
00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:40.984
After one second, ten periods have passed, and three checks have run.

61
00:03:40.984 --> 00:03:41.959
isDone is true.

62
00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:44.819
Nothing was logged, and nothing was retried.

63
00:03:44.819 --> 00:03:46.639
The schedule simply stopped.

64
00:03:47.467 --> 00:03:49.351
It's the same rule as before.

65
00:03:49.351 --> 00:03:53.573
The task goes back in the queue only when a run returns normally.

66
00:03:53.573 --> 00:04:02.214
A run that throws leaves its exception inside the ScheduledFuture, the ticket the scheduler handed you, and the task never goes back.

67
00:04:02.214 --> 00:04:06.501
The Javadoc says it plainly: subsequent executions are suppressed.

68
00:04:06.733 --> 00:04:08.984
Our cleaner didn't even leave a note.

69
00:04:08.984 --> 00:04:12.573
The only trace is in the ticket, and only get will show it.

70
00:04:12.573 --> 00:04:16.588
And hardly anyone calls get on a task that's meant to run forever.

71
00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:22.597
The fix is to catch inside the task, so no exception ever reaches the scheduler.

72
00:04:22.597 --> 00:04:26.430
Wrap the body in a try, catch RuntimeException, and log it.

73
00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:33.121
Run the demo that way, and the checks go on, about ten or eleven in the second, and isDone stays false.

74
00:04:33.933 --> 00:04:38.082
Catch RuntimeException, not Throwable, which catches everything.

75
00:04:38.082 --> 00:04:45.732
An Error, such as running out of memory, means the JVM itself is in trouble, so let it stop the task, and alert on it.

76
00:04:45.732 --> 00:04:47.677
And check isDone now and then.

77
00:04:47.677 --> 00:04:51.048
For a task that should run forever, done means dead.

78
00:04:51.767 --> 00:04:53.393
So, what's the difference?

79
00:04:53.393 --> 00:04:57.270
Fixed rate keeps the clock, and catches up after a slow round.

80
00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.709
Fixed delay keeps the rest, and moves everything later.

81
00:05:00.709 --> 00:05:06.087
And a run that throws sends the cleaner home without a word, so catch inside the task.

82
00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:07.115
Quick check.

83
00:05:07.115 --> 00:05:12.072
scheduleWithFixedDelay with a delay of 10 seconds, and every run takes 2.

84
00:05:12.072 --> 00:05:14.448
When do the first three runs start?

85
00:05:17.433 --> 00:05:19.283
At 0, 12 and 24 seconds.

86
00:05:19.283 --> 00:05:23.292
Each start waits 10 seconds after the last run ends.
